  "strategy": "1.Approachandrationale\nWe propose using a sparse representation of the step function via \u7b26\u53f7\u53d8\u5316 (\u7b26\u53f7\u53d8\u5316) in a weighted,compressed representation. The core idea is that optimal solutions often exhibit heavyweights (values close to 1) separated by larger empty zones. This approach allows us to dramatically reduce the number of parameters while maintaining solution quality. By transforming the high-dimensional vector into a sparse representation, we can:\n\n- Reduce computational time by ~50% compared to full vector processing\n- Maintain optimality by tracking the solution in terms of active indices (positions where h_i > 0.5)\n- Lessen numerical instability from large vector sizes\n\nSpecifically, we'll:\n- Represent the solution as (m,n,k) parameters, with n being the grid length\n- Keep a limited set of active indices (m << n) to minimize active crossings\n- Include weights (k) for the active points to allow continuous values\n- Maintain a transformation protocol between the dense representation and our weighted indices\n\nThis differs from previous strategies by focusing on structured,high-cardinality representations rather than continuous relaxations or kernel methods.\n\n2.Mathematicalspecification\nOur objective function C_5 is approximately represented by:\nC_5 = dx * max_lag( \u2211_{i=1}^m k_i * (1 - k_{j}) for shifted indices )\nwith constraints:\n- 0 \u2264 weights \u2264 1\n- \u2211_i weight_i = 1/dx\n- Active indices (positions with significant weight) limited to ~20 positions\n\nImplementation uses:\n- Vector: [ weight_active_indices, offsets ]\n- dx: grid spacing\n- Transformation: lexicographic sorting of active combinations\n\nRepair functions project weights back to active indices: target = summed_weights - \u2211weights, then locally transfer weight until feasible.\n\n3.Implementationprocedure\n-Start with parent value interpolation from provided initial_h_values\n-Parameterize solution as (active_indices, weight_vector)\n-Initialize active_indices pooling from grid, prioritizing robust slots\n-Use paving optimization over reduced coordinates:\n    For each lag, use blame encoding\n    Combine cheap soft constraints with direct penalty\n-Post-process to validate full grid output\n-Synchronize solution between sparse (optimized) and dense (evaluated) forms\n\n4.Searchchoicesandusefulvariations\n\u2022Default:15-20 active points, sample at 100/140/200 grid points\n\u2022 Optimization driver: \n    - Sampling: gaussian jitter around best so far\n    - Convergence:increase active points if no improvement\n    - Repulsion:ensure diversity in active indices\n\u2022 Key variation: sparse to dense ratio (0.6-0.8 active points)\n\u2022 Strategic:batch select indices based on surrogateObjective\n\n5.Computebudgetandrobustness\n\u202250samples:15s (time intensive since large grid needed)\n\u2022100samples:35s\n\u2022140samples:115s\n\u2022200samples:180s\n\u2022Schedule:run 100 if time>80s, 140 if>320s, else 100\n\u2022Termination:propose fallback if major constraint violated\n\u2022repairmechanism:balance by difference reinforcement\n\n6.Validationandreturncontract\n\u2022Check sum of weights = 1/dx (within 1e-6)\n\u20220\u2264weights\u22641\n\u2022Propagated grid values: interpolate from weights to all points\n\u2022Recompute C_5 for final dense grid\n\nThe sparse representation provides a dramatic reduction in run time while maintaining search quality. Numerical stability is ensured through vector transformation andtrimming unused dimensions automatically.",
